    "insert": "Summary:\n\nIn your study, you point to the problem that LLMs push the solution to a\nproblem too early. This has no learning value for the students. Or it\ncould also tempt students to cheat. You want to investigate how effective\nprompting is in avoiding this issue. And give students a good learning\nexperience.\n\nTo do this, you want to evaluate publicly available LLMs, namely GPT 4.0\nand Gemini 1.5, with prompting strategies for the ability to limit output and\nhide solutions.\n\nAs a first step, you will evaluate 10 different prompts and test the models for\ntheir ability to provide students with learning steps. Resistance to\njailbreaking will also be tested.\n\nIn a second step, you will take these prompts and create a learning environment\nfor computer science students to solve predefined problems. There will also be\na control group using only textbooks to solve the same problems. All students\nwill be surveyed at the end.\n\nTitle:\n\nYour title is appropriate and aligns with your focus on researching the\neffectiveness of providing learning steps without revealing the solution too\nearly, using LLMs.\n\nHook/Anker/Teaser:\n\nYour expose effectively begins by citing studies that demonstrate the relevance\nof your research to students, noting that 50\\% are already using LLMs for\nlearning. You highlight issues like cheating and teacher shortages, to state\nyour goal to address these challenges by using LLMs to create a learning\nexperience that discourages cheating and provides personalized learning. You\nidentify the issue with LLMs and their tendency to provide solutions too\nquickly. This is an interesting question to try to answer. Additionally, you\nmention that no prior evaluation was done on the systems (\"No evaluation has\nbeen done on the readiness with which these systems will tell students\nsolutions if asked directly and if this has a negative effect on learning.\").\nBut this statement will be difficult to prove. So you might want to rephrase\nthis sentence to something like \"There is almost no prior evaluation of how\nwillingly, these systems provide solutions when asked directly and whether this\nhas a negative impact on learning.\".\n\nResearch Questions:\n\nYour research questions are well-chosen. They are a good foundation for your\nstudy and align well with your title. However, the sub-questions are yes/no\nquestions. You might want to rephrase them as \"How do students prefer LLMs that\ndo or do not provide solutions?\" and \"How do these systems enhance learning\ncompared to existing LLM assistants?\" to provide a wider range in which you can\nanswer these questions.\n\nSOTA/Theoretical Framework:\n\nIn your approach, you identify a researchable gap. That is feasible in the time\nframe. Next, you explain what an LLMs is. Since your study heavily relies\non LLMs it might be beneficent to include some criticism on the\ntraining on these models to inform the reader. They are trained on large\ndatasets that are not publicly available, and therefore it is not transparent\nhow a LLMs came to the output it generated.\n\nIn the next part, you mention that the quality of the responses of LLMs depend\non prompting techniques, and that you would like to explore specialty this\npart. But you never mention the exact techniques you want to use. You also\nmention these novel prompting techniques later in your methodology and do not\ngo into detail. You should clarify what you mean by that.\n\nAt the end of your framework, you mention two issues with LLMs and how you plan\nto address them in your study. Namely, jailbreaking and hallucinations. I think\nyour strategies in this part are good.\n\nMethodology:\n\nYour methodology outlines using 10 different prompt to instruct LLMs in\nproviding good teaching steps to student. This will be using in the testing\nphase. But you should clarify whether a single prompt will be used for all\nexperiments or if the best-performing prompt will be used. Also while you\nmention groups of five, the exact roles or configurations of these groups are\nunclear. It would help to specify whether each LLM will have a regular group\nand a control group, as seems implied. Additionally, clarifying what you mean\nby \"variance for each tool\" in the last sentence would also be beneficial.\n\nYour chosen demographic, computer science students, could present challenges.\nYou plan to collaborate with teachers to assess students learning levels, but\nuniversity teachers often lack the detailed knowledge of individual students\nlearning level due to independent nature of university learning. A more\nsuitable demographic would be school students, or it would require extensive\nresearch on the current learning level. Which could be difficult in this\nlimited time frame.\n\nFinding participants without prior exposure to LLMs may also be difficult,\nespecially among computer science students, who are likely to have used these\ntools before. Instead, consider including students with varying levels of\nexperience for a broader analysis\n\nThe sample size is small but manageable in the limited time frame. You also\nmention that. However, you should outline a strategy for recruiting\nparticipants and specify plans in case you are not able to recruit the required\nnumber of students.\n\nIt is good that you address how outliers, hallucinations, and potential\njailbreaks will be handled.\n\nLanguage:\n\nThe language of your expose is good, and I did not notice any grammatically\nissues or errors\n\nSchedule:\n\nYour schedule is mostly well planned. However, you indent to prepare questions\nabout student's current knowledge levels before recruitment. This can lead to\noverly general questions. And it would be more appropriate to reverse the order\nto more effectively tailor the questions to the students. Since you plan to\nincorporate the current student's knowledge base, with help of teachers, into\nthe questions.\n\n"
